lesson "Limits, Briefly" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Calculus Essentials"
  difficulty: "Intermediate"
  icon: "ChevronRight"
  summary: "A short recap of the one idea calculus is built on: what a function approaches, as opposed to what it does when it gets there."
  skills: ["lim-notation", "lim-algebraic", "lim-at-infinity"]

  slide "Approaching, Not Arriving" {
    id: "ce1-hook"
    cat: "The Idea"
    > A limit asks what a function **heads towards** as the input closes in on some
    > value. Crucially, it does not care what happens exactly at that value, or
    > whether anything happens there at all.
    >
    > $$\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L$$
    >
    > The classic case is $f(x) = \dfrac{x^2 - 1}{x - 1}$ at $x = 1$. Substituting
    > gives $\tfrac00$, which is meaningless. But factor first:
    >
    > $$\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1} = x + 1 \quad \text{for every } x \neq 1$$
    >
    > So as $x$ approaches $1$, the function approaches $2$. There is a hole in the
    > graph at exactly $x = 1$, and the limit sails straight over it.
    >
    > Drag the point towards the hole and watch the height.

    scene plane {
      x: [-1, 3.5]
      y: [-0.5, 4]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 0.4 { range: [-0.5, 2.5], step: 0.02 }
      curve f = x + 1 { color: primary, width: 3 }
      point hole = (1, 2) { open, color: danger, r: 8 }
      point probe = (t, t + 1) { drag: x -> t, color: accent, r: 8 }
      line drop = (t, t + 1) -> (t, 0) { color: accent, style: dashed, width: 2 }
      label at (-0.8, 3.7) = "x = ${t}, f(x) = ${t + 1}" { color: accent }
      label at (1.15, 2.15) = "undefined at x = 1" { color: danger }
      slider t { label: "slide towards 1" }
    }

    goal "Get within 0.05 of x = 1 and see what the height is doing" {
      when: abs(t - 1) < 0.05
      hint: "Creep up on it from either side. The height heads for 2 without ever landing there."
    }
  }

  slide "How to Actually Evaluate One" {
    id: "ce1-evaluate"
    cat: "Three Techniques"
    > Three techniques cover nearly everything you will meet.
    >
    > **Substitute.** Try it first. If $f(a)$ is defined and the function is
    > continuous there, that is the answer. Most limits are this dull, and that is a
    > good thing.
    >
    > **Factor and cancel.** If substituting gives $\tfrac00$, there is a common
    > factor hiding. Cancel it and substitute again.
    >
    > **Rationalise.** When square roots produce the $\tfrac00$, multiply top and
    > bottom by the conjugate, exactly as with complex numbers.
    >
    > $\tfrac00$ is not an answer. It is a signal that the expression needs
    > rearranging before it will tell you anything.

    order {
      ask "Put the steps in order for evaluating $\lim_{x \to 3} \dfrac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3}$."
      skill: "lim-algebraic"
      item "Substitute $x = 3$ and get $\tfrac00$, so more work is needed"
      item "Factor the top as a difference of squares: $(x-3)(x+3)$"
      item "Cancel the common $(x - 3)$ to leave $x + 3$"
      item "Substitute again: $3 + 3 = 6$"
      decoy "Conclude the limit does not exist"
      ! "$6$. The $\tfrac00$ meant 'rearrange me', not 'give up'. Cancelling is legitimate because $x$ only ever approaches $3$ and never equals it, so $x - 3$ is never actually zero."
    }
  }

  slide "Limits at Infinity" {
    id: "ce1-infinity"
    cat: "Three Techniques"
    > The other question worth asking is what happens as $x$ grows without bound.
    >
    > The technique: **divide top and bottom by the highest power of $x$ present**,
    > then use the fact that $\dfrac{1}{x} \to 0$.
    >
    > $$\lim_{x\to\infty} \frac{3x^2 + 5x}{x^2 - 1} = \lim_{x\to\infty} \frac{3 + 5/x}{1 - 1/x^2} = \frac{3}{1} = 3$$
    >
    > Every term with an $x$ underneath dies away, leaving only the ratio of the
    > leading coefficients. In practice you can often read the answer straight off:
    > **compare the highest powers.** Equal powers give the ratio of coefficients, a
    > bigger power on top runs off to infinity, and a bigger power underneath drives
    > it to zero.

    numeric {
      ask "What is $\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{6x^2 + 2x}{2x^2 + 9}$?"
      skill: "lim-at-infinity"
      answer: 3
      expect: 6/2
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Same highest power top and bottom, so take the ratio of the leading coefficients."
      ! "$6/2 = 3$. The $2x$ and the $9$ are irrelevant once $x$ is enormous — they are swamped by the squared terms."
    }
  }

  slide "Why Any of This Matters" {
    id: "ce1-why"
    cat: "The Payoff"
    > Limits look like a technicality until you see what they are for. **The
    > derivative is a limit**, and it is a $\tfrac00$ one:
    >
    > $$f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}$$
    >
    > Put $h = 0$ straight in and you get $\tfrac00$. The whole of differentiation is
    > the business of doing that cancellation properly, once and for all, so you never
    > have to again.
    >
    > That is why limits come first. Every rule in the next lesson was obtained by
    > evaluating exactly this expression.

    quiz {
      ask "Why can you not just substitute $h = 0$ into the derivative definition?"
      skill: "lim-notation"
      * "It gives $\tfrac00$, which is meaningless until the expression is rearranged"
      - "Because $h$ is not allowed to be zero in mathematics" { why: "Zero is a perfectly good number. The problem is dividing by it, which this expression would do." }
      - "You can, and it gives zero" { why: "The top is also zero, so it is $\tfrac00$, not $\tfrac0{\text{something}}$. Those are very different situations." }
      hint "Put $h = 0$ into both the numerator and the denominator and see what you get."
      ! "Both parts go to zero at once. Cancelling an $h$ first is what makes it evaluable, and that cancellation is where every differentiation rule comes from."
    }
  }
}
